The Proxy Design Pattern is a structural design pattern that provides a placeholder or surrogate for another object to control access to it.

🧠 Concept
A Proxy acts as a middleman:

It controls access to the real object.

It can add logic like lazy initialization, logging, access control, caching, remote communication, etc.

✅ Real-World Analogy
Imagine a credit card as a proxy for your bank account:

You don’t give your bank account directly to the merchant.

Instead, the credit card acts as an intermediary to validate and limit access.

📌 Common Types of Proxy
Type	Purpose
Virtual Proxy	Lazy initialization (e.g., loading a big image)
Protection Proxy	Access control (e.g., admin vs user)
Remote Proxy	Represent objects in different JVMs/machines
Caching Proxy	Cache results to avoid expensive operations
Logging Proxy	Add logging functionality

