Spring boot AOP logic based on Proxy DP

@Aspect
@Component
public class LoggingAspect {

    @Around("execution(* com.example.demo.PaymentService.*(..))")
    public Object logExecutionTime(ProceedingJoinPoint joinPoint) throws Throwable {
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();

        Object result = joinPoint.proceed(); // call the original method

        long timeTaken = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
        System.out.println(joinPoint.getSignature() + " executed in " + timeTaken + "ms");

        return result;
    }
}

✅ What's Happening Under the Hood?
Spring creates a proxy object of PaymentService at runtime:

You inject PaymentService, but you're actually calling a proxy.

This proxy wraps the real method with custom behavior (like logging).

🧩 Notes
Spring uses JDK dynamic proxies (if interface-based) or CGLIB proxies (if class-based).

This is a perfect Proxy Design Pattern in action — Spring adds behavior without modifying the actual class.